const faqs = [
  {
    q: "How do you prepare for a trade show?",
    a: "Start six weeks out, and start with the attendee list rather than the booth. Book meetings with the specific people you want to meet, announce where you will be in your email program and on LinkedIn, and build the booth around one thing worth stopping for. Then brief the crew on who you are trying to meet and what to ask them. The booth itself is the easiest part to get right and the least decisive."
  },
  {
    q: "How fast should trade show follow-up happen?",
    a: "Inside five business days. Figure you have about a week while a prospect can still put a face to your company name, and after that you are one badge scan among dozens. Send whatever you promised at the booth first, before anything promotional, then sort the rest of the contacts into now, later, and never."
  },
  {
    q: "What should a small booth do differently from a big one?",
    a: "Stop trying to win on square footage, because you cannot. One clear thing worth stopping for beats a large empty backdrop, and the meetings you book before the show matter more than where your booth sits on the floor plan. A ten-by-ten with eight booked meetings will outperform an island booth running on walk-up traffic."
  },
  {
    q: "Are trade shows worth it for precast and AEC companies?",
    a: "Yes, when they are treated as a campaign instead of a three-day event. The precast and AEC world still decides a great deal of its business in rooms like these, face to face, near a product you can touch. A booth that stands alone, with no work before or after it, is hard to defend. The same booth in the middle of an eight-week campaign usually is not."
  }
];
