  <Prose tag="The case" heading="Why do most booths not pay back?">
    <p>Booths fail to pay back for a reason that has nothing to do with the booth. The spend concentrates on the three days of the show, while the work that decides the outcome sits on either side of them. The registration, the space, the drayage, the hotel rooms, the crew's week: all of it buys the same thing, which is access to a room full of the right people for three days. Access is not a result. The result comes from what happened before those people arrived and what happens after they go home.</p>
    <p>Look at what the fee actually purchases. For three days, the producers, specifiers, engineers, and owners you spend the rest of the year trying to reach are walking a single concrete floor, wearing name tags, expecting to be talked to. That concentration is the entire value of the event. It is also perishable. It exists for three days and then it scatters back across the country.</p>
    <p>An exhibitor who shows up without appointments is betting the whole spend on aisle traffic and a nice backdrop. Some of that bet pays. A few good walk-ups happen at every show. But walk-ups are luck, meetings booked in advance are not, and the difference between the two is six weeks of unglamorous work with an attendee list and a phone.</p>
    <p>The quieter failure is the week after. The badge scans land in a spreadsheet, everyone digs out from the work that piled up while they were gone, and ten days pass. The prospect who asked a real question at your booth has since talked to a dozen other vendors and can no longer place your face. Exhibitors let the follow-up die exactly this way, year after year, which is why the few who move fast look so good by comparison. The show itself is the middle of the campaign, never the campaign.</p>
  </Prose>

  <Steps
    tag="Sequence of operations"
    heading="The campaign in three phases"
    intro="Three phases, one campaign. The middle phase gets nearly all the budget and attention. The outer two decide the return."
    wide={true}
    steps={[
      {
        id: 'BEFORE',
        title: "Six weeks out",
        copy: "Pull the attendee and exhibitor lists and book meetings with the people you already want to know: the specifier you have been chasing, the producer who buys what you sell, the engineer who called once and went quiet. Announce where you will be in your email program and on LinkedIn, with your booth number, so the people who already know you can find you. Build the booth around one thing worth stopping for, a real product, a cutaway, a working demonstration, something a visitor cannot see on a website. Then brief the crew in writing: who we want to meet, what we ask them, and what we hand them."
      },
      {
        id: 'DURING',
        title: "The three days",
        copy: "Work the list, not the aisle traffic. The meetings you booked are the show, and the walk-ups are a bonus on top. Capture every real conversation in writing the same day, while you still remember what the person asked and what you promised, because by the last morning the badges blur together. Photograph the booth and the people in it, with their permission, since those photos feed a month of posts afterward. And give one crew member an hour to walk the floor with no agenda except listening, because the aisles are where you hear what the industry is worried about this year."
      },
      {
        id: 'AFTER',
        title: "The week after",
        copy: "Follow up inside five business days, while they can still put a face to your company name. Sort every contact into three piles, now, later, and never, and be honest about the never pile so it does not clog the other two. Send the thing you promised first, the drawing, the sample, the introduction, before anything promotional goes out. Then move the later pile into your email program, so the show keeps paying for months instead of ending when the carpet comes up."
      }
    ]}
  />

  <Prose tag="Field notes" heading="The one-metric test" mist={true} marks={true}>
    <p>Before you book next year's space, count one number: the conversations from this year's show that turned into a meeting, a quote, or a spec discussion. Not badge scans. Not booth traffic. Not how busy the crew felt on day two. Conversations that went somewhere.</p>
    <p>Count them because memory inflates. A crowded booth feels like a good show six months later even when nothing came of the crowd, and a slow booth that produced three serious quote conversations was probably worth the trip. The number is the only honest witness, and it is worth writing down within a month of the show, while the trail from conversation to outcome is still visible.</p>
    <p>If the number is low, the instinct is to blame the booth and buy a bigger one next year. Resist that. A low number almost always points at the phases on either side: no meetings booked going in, no follow-up going out inside the week. Fix those two things and run the same booth again before you spend more on square footage.</p>
  </Prose>
</GuideSheet>
